#X text 172 352 <-- go to head of list (click first);
#X text 109 439 <-- bang when we reach the end of the list;
#N canvas 692 31 461 538 data02 1;
#X scalar triangle01 174 190 43 89 75 \;;
#X scalar triangle01 180 442 -48 161 505 \;;
#X restore 472 435 pd data02;
#X obj 39 715 pointer;
#X text 25 166 Once you have managed to attach a [pointer] to a particular scalar \, you can read out the various fields either via the [get] object or by sending messages directly to [pointer]., f 74;
#X text 25 215 You can send the [pointer] object a "traverse" message to point it to the head of the list. The argument "pd-data02" indicates the Pd window named "data2." The head of the list means \, not the first scalar in the list \, but the position before the first scalar \, which is a valid pointer in Pd but has no data or template., f 74;
#X msg 39 654 traverse pd-data02 \, next \, get x y dx dy color;
#X listbox 85 742 20 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X msg 51 683 traverse pd-data02 \, next 2 \, get x y dx dy color;
#X obj 39 773 print left-outlet;
#X text 92 385 <-- output next item (click 3 times to reach the end);
#X text 292 510 and outputs the values of the requested fields, f 23;
#X obj 39 496 get triangle01 x y color;
#X text 222 495 <-- [get] takes incoming pointers;
#X text 99 412 <-- iterates \, outputting pointers to scalars;
#X text 27 293 The "next" message tells the [pointer] object to go to the next scalar in the list and output a pointer to it. If we reach the end of the list \, [pointer] outputs a bang at the right outlet., f 74;
#X msg 39 353 traverse pd-data02;
#X text 459 840 updated for Pd version 0.57;
#X text 25 110 From this example onward we'll put scalars in their own separate window ("data02") - this will allow us to use the name of the window to indicate to [pointer] objects where to look for scalars to attach themselves to., f 74;
#X text 26 12 The scalars in any particular window \, such as "data02" here \, are kept in a particular order. The [pointer] and [vpointer] objects allow you to iteratively scan through the scalars in order. Each [(v)pointer] can be attached to some scalar \, or can be unattached \, or can be attached to the beginning of a window before any of the scalars in it (the "head"). Different [pointer] objects might be attached to the same scalar \, or not., f 74;
#X text 35 555 The [get] object takes a pointer \, checks that its structure agrees with what [get] is expecting \, i.e. \, "triangle01" \, and if so outputs the values of x \, y \, and color in the usual right to left order. If the requested structure is given as a hyphen ("-") \, any structure is allowed \, although the field names should still be defined for whatever pointer comes in., f 74;
#X text 375 654 You can combi;
#X text 230 741 <-- note that the values come out as a list to the right-hand outlet (the left-hand one sends out pointers.);
#X text 169 773 A pointer sent in a message is an elementary Pd type on a level with "float" and "symbol" \, but its true value can't be printed or saved to a file., f 54;
#N canvas 802 174 562 708 triangle01-again 0;
#X obj 43 291 filledpolygon color color 1 0 0 dx dy(-1000:1000)(1000:-1000) 20 0;
#X obj 42 568 drawnumber dy dx(-1000:1000)(-995:1005) dy(-1000:1000)(1000:-1000) 0 dy=;
#X obj 42 608 drawnumber dx dx(-1000:1000)(-995:1005) dy(-1000:1000)(985:-1015) 0 dx=;
#X obj 42 648 drawnumber color dx(-1000:1000)(-995:1005) dy(-1000:1000)(970:-1030) 0 color=;
#X obj 43 333 filledpolygon 0 0 1 dx(-1000:1000)(-996:1004) dy(-1000:1000)(996:-1004) dx(-1000:1000)(-1004:996) dy(-1000:1000)(996:-1004) dx(-1000:1000)(-1004:996) dy(-1000:1000)(1004:-996) dx(-1000:1000)(-996:1004) dy(-1000:1000)(1004:-996) dx(-1000:1000)(-996:1004) dy(-1000:1000)(996:-1004), f 28;
#X obj 42 520 drawnumber x -5 0 0 x=;
#X obj 42 544 drawnumber y -5 12 0 y=;
#X obj 44 170 struct triangle01 float x float y float dx float dy float color, f 11;
#X text 36 20 We repeat the definition of "struct triangle01" from the previous example. Any patch using a structure should contain a separate definition of it \, so that Pure Data will be able to manipulate scalars belonging to it. But note: if you have two "structs" with the same name but different fields \, for instance if they're in different patches open at the same time \, there will be problems.;
